    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nRare 1977 Pontiac Can Am Emerges After 30 Years in a Barn, It's an Unrestored Survivor\n14 photos\nPhoto: Craiglist\nThe Malaise Era was in full swing in 1977. Muscle cars had been stripped off their potent V8 engines in the early years of that decade, and some nameplates had been discontinued for good. The Pontiac GTO , the vehicle credited for having popularized the muscle car, was one of those cars.\nWith the second-gen model gone after the 1972 model year, the GTO returned as a heavily restyled mid-size car with the \"Colonnade\" body style. But Pontiac sold only 4,806 units and decided to slap the GTO badge on a compact Ventura in 1974. The latter was a tad more successful with 7,058 examples sold, but Pontiac opted to send the nameplate into the history books.\nThe company revisited the three years later. It did not revive the GTO but debuted a new performance-oriented model that was also based on the Le Mans. It was called the Can Am, after the then-famous Can-Am racing series, thus continuing the race theme that Pontiac used for the Trans Am and Grand Prix.\nThe Can Am was the brainchild of Jim Wangers, the man behind the more iconic GTO. Designed to revive the spirit of the original muscle car, the Can Am was based on the LeMans Sport Coupe but used the shaker hood scoop of the Trans Am, while its ducktail spoiler was based on the Grand Am All-American concept car.\nCameo White was the only available exterior color, but the Can Am also got a unique livery with tri-tone stripes in orange, yellow, and gold. More importantly, it was fitted with the W72 400-cubic-inch (6.6-liter) V8 engine from the Trans Am. The mill delivered 200 horsepower, 20 more than the similar unit offered in the LeMans Sport Coupe.\nAlthough 200 horsepower wasn't much compared to the golden-era GTO, it was a pretty solid figure in 1977. Only the Chevrolet Corvette delivered more oomph (210 horsepower), with most of the Can Am's competitors coming with far less on tap. Some cars sold in California were also equipped with a 403-cubic-inch Oldsmobile V8 rated at 185 horsepower.\nPontiac envisioned the Can Am as a limited-edition vehicle, but production was cut shorter than expected when the mold used to produce the fiberglass rear spoiler broke. Because the LeMans was scheduled to be downsized for 1978, Pontiac opted to discontinue the Can Am altogether. Only 1,377 examples were produced.\nIt's unclear how many of these cars have survived, but I've seen a few pop up for sale in recent years. Some have been restored, while others became rust buckets due to improper storage. The Can Am you see here is somewhere in between. It spent about three decades off the road, but it's not too far gone. Moreover, it's an unaltered survivor.\nHailing from Olathe, Kansas, the Can Am was parked in a barn in 1995 with 106,000 miles (170,590 km) on the clock. The shell has quite a few rust holes, while much of the upholstery is damaged, but it's pretty obvious that this Pontiac was parked in all-original condition .\nIt still has the factory W72 V8 engine under the hood, and the good news here is that the drivetrain has been revived. Both the powerplant and the automatic transmission work as they should, per the seller. The vehicle is also loaded with options and has never been involved in a crash.\nIf it's the type of project car you've been looking for, the Can Am is available for $11,000 . For reference, examples in very good condition can fetch anywhere between $25,000 and $40,000, while concours-level cars can trade for over $50,000.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nclassic car Pontiac pontiac can am Malaise era barn find rusty\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Range Rover Sport Costs Less Than a New Base Toyota Corolla, Would You Buy It?",
